### Redshift Evolution Test

**Prediction:** If dark energy suppresses merger activity at low
redshift, the disturbed/relaxed ratio should be >1 at high redshift
(matter dominated, more mergers) and converge toward 1.0 at low
redshift (dark energy dominated, merger suppression).

| Redshift | N superclusters | Mean ratio | Median ratio | vs 1.0 |
|----------|----------------|-----------|--------------|--------|
| z=[0.0,0.1) | 73 | 0.979±0.111 | 0.500 | p=0.855 — balanced |
| z=[0.1,0.2) | 152 | 1.272±0.085 | 0.833 | p=0.002 |
| z=[0.2,0.3) | 128 | 1.367±0.091 | 1.000 | p=0.0001 |
| z=[0.3,0.5) | 97 | 1.657±0.109 | 2.000 | p<0.0001 |
| z=[0.5,0.8) | 18 | 1.556±0.209 | 1.750 | p=0.019 |

Trend with redshift: slope=0.937, R²=0.693, p=0.080
Higher redshift = more disturbed — consistent with prediction.

**Important caveat:** The redshift evolution of merger rate is
established science — more mergers at high redshift is expected from
standard structure formation. This confirms it with a new method but
does not constitute a new discovery. The novel result is the degree
of balance at low redshift and the uniformity of morphology
distribution across superclusters.

