## Physical Interpretation

**What the result is:**

Every supercluster samples the full cluster morphology distribution
proportionally. No supercluster is systematically biased toward
disturbed or relaxed populations. The median supercluster has exactly
equal numbers of clusters above and below the global morphology median.

The cosmic web distributes cluster dynamical states like a perfectly
shuffled deck — more evenly than any of 10,000 random universes tested.

**What the result is NOT:**

This is not simply because superclusters contain mixed populations —
that would be trivially true by definition. It is because the mixing
is MORE PERFECT than random chance produces. The excess of balanced
superclusters is 4.29σ above random expectation.

It is also NOT evidence that violent mergers and pristine cool cores
specifically pair up — the physical threshold test rules that out.

**Connection to dark energy:**

The ISW morphology signal (Parks 2026, main paper) exists precisely at
z=[0.10,0.15) — the epoch of dark energy domination. The supercluster
balance result shows median ratio 1.000 at low redshift, climbing to
>1.5 at high redshift — consistent with dark energy suppressing merger
activity and driving the system toward equilibrium.

Together these suggest dark energy may function as a dynamical
equilibrium mechanism — not merely a geometric expansion term but a
regulator of the merger/relaxation cycle within large scale structure.

If dark energy is a conservation mechanism, its equation of state
w = −1 (cosmological constant) represents the exact equilibrium value.
Deviations from w = −1 would correspond to epochs or environments
where the balance is broken.

**The physical picture:**

A supercluster filament has a fixed total mass budget. Most clusters
sit in a middling dynamical state — neither violently merging nor
pristine cool cores. Dark energy sets the expansion rate which
determines infall timescales. The cosmic web distributes the full
range of dynamical states uniformly across all superclusters —
every filament gets its proportional share of the merger/relaxation
cycle regardless of environment.

